   Properties: {'session_id': 'ceeb15de-be05-4191-92e3-ade330d6381d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5170357315809222, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/objectives-and-data/browse-objectives/health-care-access-and-quality', 'https://ahimafoundation.ahima.org/research/the-critical-role-of-web-accessibility-in-health-information-access-understanding-and-use/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08010260562044232, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that simply making information more accessible will automatically and passively improve healthcare professionals\' functioning, regardless of their deliberate engagement. This reasoning overlooks the necessity of active processing, comprehension, and application of information for professional performance to improve. The assumption that passive exposure alone leads to significant improvements in diagnostic accuracy and care coordination is not logically justified within the explanation and ignores potential barriers such as information overload or lack of motivation. Therefore, the causal chain presented is flawed and oversimplified.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7654694837439076, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.660195521477879, 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes the functioning of healthcare professionals to improve because the mere presence of abundant information in the clinical environment leads professionals to subconsciously absorb relevant knowledge, even without actively engaging with it. This passive exposure to information is sufficient to enhance diagnostic accuracy, treatment decisions, and care coordination, as healthcare professionals intuitively internalize best practices through their environment. Therefore,