   Properties: {'session_id': 'ceeb15de-be05-4191-92e3-ade330d6381d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7735919025677318, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10573559270036455, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation asserts that neurochemical changes from chronic addiction \\"directly interfere with the absorption and effectiveness of therapeutic interventions,\\" making therapy physically less effective and thus reducing compliance. However, this reasoning conflates the physiological effectiveness of therapy with the patient\'s willingness or motivation to comply. Decreased effectiveness of therapy might reduce motivation, but the argument that neurochemical states cause a \\"biological incompatibility\\" with therapy compliance is not fully established or internally coherent. The explanation does not clearly differentiate between compliance (a behavioral choice) and therapy effectiveness (an outcome), and the causal link between brain changes and motivation to comply is not explicitly or logically detailed.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7091397753903512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6498646876417895, 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads to decreased therapy compliance primarily because individuals with chronic addiction are exposed to neurochemical changes that directly interfere with the absorption and effectiveness of therapeutic interventions. As the severity of ch