o increase cognitive alertness and motivate individuals to actively seek out and retain health knowledge as a means of alleviating their discomfort. Therefore, higher levels of feelings of fear and shame should lead to improved health literacy, as these emotions drive a proactive engagement with health resources and educational materials.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.005369032657953901, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8456222445335322, 'min_prob': 0.136425530088776, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7990390959408848, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0042046912653529245, 'prob_variance': 0.06730488412750603, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 7.782586799701699e-06, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30477662181492116, 'avg_nll': 0.2511440336628612, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1981720531427167, 'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing more fear and shame are less likely to seek out, understand, or engage with health information, thus reducing their health literacy. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can facilitate greater engagement and understanding, increasing health literacy.', 'prob_std': 0.25943184871465963, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.2854952255835204, 'max_window_entropy': 1.1941344520847426}

