   Properties: {'session_id': 'ceeb15de-be05-4191-92e3-ade330d6381d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5087932153675168, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8768475/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08684776474186481, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation suggests that increased knowledge leads to greater feelings of fear and shame, which in turn reduces future knowledge acquisition. However, the relationship being assessed is between feelings of fear and shame (source) and knowledge (target), not the other way around. The explanation primarily describes a reverse causal direction (knowledge \\u2192 fear/shame \\u2192 less knowledge), rather than directly explaining how higher initial fear and shame cause lower knowledge. There is some implicit reasoning that heightened fear and shame discourage knowledge acquisition, but this is not clearly or directly articulated.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7169012894713833, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7116085075461966, 'motivation': 'A negative relationship between feelings of fear and shame and knowledge can be explained by the notion that as individuals accumulate more knowledge, they become increasingly aware of the complexities and potential pitfalls in various domains, which in turn heightens their feelings of fear and shame when confronted with challenging tasks or unfamiliar information. Thus, greater knowledge directly amplifies feelings of fear and shame, leading to a reduction in future knowledge acquisition as individuals seek to avoid these uncomfortable emotions, establishing an inverse causal relationship.', 'aggre