   Properties: {'session_id': 'ceeb15de-be05-4191-92e3-ade330d6381d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4679916716662003, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399121005747'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08680612682454796, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation describes how higher health literacy increases awareness and perception of language and cultural barriers, implying that greater health literacy is associated with a heightened recognition (and possibly experience) of these barriers. This suggests a positive, not negative, relationship: as health literacy increases, so does the perception of barriers. However, the relationship type is labeled as NEGATIVE, which would require an explanation showing that higher health literacy reduces language and cultural barriers. Thus, the causal reasoning in the explanation does not logically support the specified negative relationship.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7894301296425903, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7142594518183849, 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to become more aware of, and sensitive to, language and cultural differences within healthcare settings. As people with greater health literacy are more likely to recognize nuances in communication and cultural expectations, they may perceive language and cultural barriers as more significant compared