   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 0.9289179600564148, 'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where Proactive attitude causes Time or Time causes Proactive attitude. Without clear definitions or a specific context, neither variable can be shown to causally influence the other. They are conceptually unrelated in terms of direct cause and effect.', 'session_id': 'ceeb15de-be05-4191-92e3-ade330d6381d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.2699278779503278, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.05567378828445862, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation conflates subjective perception of time with the objective passage of time. While being proactive and engaged may make time feel as if it passes more quickly, it does not cause actual days to become shorter. The reasoning is flawed because it mistakes changes in perception for changes in reality.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7925072866878107, 'motivation': 'Having a proactive attitude causes time to pass more quickly, because when you take initiative and stay engaged, your perception of time speeds up. This means that proactive people actually experience days that are shorter than those of less proactive individuals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a proactive attitude causes time to pass more quickly, because when you take initiative and stay engaged, your perception of time speeds up. This means that proactive people actually experience days that are shorter than those of less proactive individuals.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.07474020400520642, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.8497898183807888, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 4.3978642691377735e-05, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2