   Properties: {'session_id': 'ceeb15de-be05-4191-92e3-ade330d6381d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8320842646452962, 'citations': ['https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanhl/article/PIIS2666-7568(23)00263-5/fulltext', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/0898264320923245', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2260134125000222'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10313030949360193, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation logically connects increased willingness of a social network to assist with an increase in dependency, which is then linked to decreased activity and therefore greater frailty. It also posits that less willingness leads to greater self-reliance and reduced frailty. While this chain of reasoning is internally consistent, it relies on the assumption that assistance causes decreased activity rather than enabling or supporting independence, which may not always be the case. The direction of causality is plausible but not the only possible interpretation, and the explanation does not consider alternative mechanisms or potential confounders. However, within the internal logic provided, the reasoning is coherent and plausible.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7402800933618633, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6130512696850625, 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of social network to assist may directly decrease frailty by fostering a sense of dependency, which in turn leads individuals to engage less frequently in 