   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2068683104923599, 'session_id': '5cdbfe3d-f90f-4104-8143-cb756be353f5', 'judge_perplexity': 1.735213767703032, 'prob_std': 0.2871590512186274, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.1038001192817393, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6904940970207646, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that there is no direct causal relationship between care avoidance behavior and the functioning of healthcare professionals. It provides reasoning that a direct link is absent because care avoidance by individuals does not immediately or directly alter healthcare professionals\' functioning\\u2014such impacts would be mediated by factors like workload or patient outcomes. The explanation also acknowledges that these mediating variables are accounted for elsewhere (\\"already present in the CLD\\"), justifying the absence of a direct link. This reasoning is internally consistent, clearly distinguishes between direct and indirect effects, and avoids overattributing causality. It logically supports the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism connecting the two variables without mediation.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6489007414599604, 'motivation': "Care avoidance behavior does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which an individual's avoidance of care immediately and directly alters the functioning (performance, well-being, or capacity) of healthcare professionals. Any effect would likely be mediated by other variables such as workload or