   Properties: {'session_id': '5cdbfe3d-f90f-4104-8143-cb756be353f5', 'judge_perplexity': 58505037995518.57, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9073389/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/12/5/e056466', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09833349119226598, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that care avoidance behavior causes an increase in financial barriers. It argues that when individuals avoid care, they miss out on preventive services, leading to higher long-term costs. It then posits that healthcare systems respond by increasing out-of-pocket costs to compensate for reduced utilization, thereby raising financial barriers. Conversely, it claims that decreased care avoidance (i.e., more people accessing care) leads to lower costs due to economies of scale, thus reducing financial barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.8060535029705603, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6304853400527383, 'motivation': 'An increase in care avoidance behavior leads to a rise in financial barriers, as individuals who avoid care are less likely to take advantage of preventive services or early interventions that could reduce overall healthcare expenditures. As more people avoid care, healthcare systems respond by increasing out-of-pocket costs and fees to compensate for reduced utilization, thereby amplifying financial barriers. Conversely, when care avoidance behavior decreases, heightened service utilization drives down costs through economies of scale, thus diminishing financial barriers. This dynamic illustrates a direct relationship between care avoidance behavior and financial barriers.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in care avoi