   Properties: {'session_id': '5cdbfe3d-f90f-4104-8143-cb756be353f5', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6675713421817953, 'citations': ['https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.091194340411382, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The stated relationship type is \\"NEGATIVE,\\" which means the explanation should argue that a healthy lifestyle leads to decreased acute care demand and fewer ED visits. However, the explanation claims the opposite: it argues that a healthy lifestyle could increase ED visits due to heightened health awareness and vigilance, leading individuals to seek care for minor symptoms. This is an internally coherent causal mechanism\\u2014greater health vigilance could plausibly lead to more medical visits\\u2014but it supports a positive (not negative) relationship between the source and target variable. The explanation does not logically support the negative relationship specified in the relationship type. Thus, the reasoning is internally consistent but does not match the required direction of causality."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7443172945041461, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6044639674711215, 'motivation': 'A healthy lifestyle can increase acute care demand leading to an ED visit due to heightened health awareness and vigilance among individuals who prioritize their well-being. Those engaged in a healthy lifestyle may be more likely to recognize and respond to minor or ambiguous symptoms, interpreting them as potential health risks. This proactive approach can drive a higher propensity to seek immediate medical attention, resulting in increased a