1105. Willingness of social network-[NONE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.301287475394183, 'session_id': '5cdbfe3d-f90f-4104-8143-cb756be353f5', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9453183929659947, 'prob_std': 0.31205197104053706, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11426514672813382, 'citations': ['https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/0963721417730833', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-network-theory', 'https://dlab.berkeley.edu/news/concepts-and-measurements-social-network-analysis'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6940352754415157, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that the \'willingness of social network\'\\u2014which refers to the readiness or enthusiasm of network members to engage or participate\\u2014does not directly cause the \'size of social network,\' which is defined as the number of members in the network. It further clarifies that while willingness may influence qualities like activeness or supportiveness, it does not in itself add members to the network; thus, other factors are responsible for determining size. This reasoning is internally consistent: willingness is about the attitude or behavior of current members, not about recruiting or adding new members. Unless willingness somehow influences recruitment, there is no direct causal link. The explanation clearly differentiates between attributes of existing members and the structural size of the network.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.597788712777631, 'motivation': "'Willingness of social network' does not cause 'Size of social network' directly. While a more willing network may be more active or supportive, it does not directly increase the number of members (size). The number of people in a network is determined by other factors, not the willin