   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3608176805017012, 'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing therapy compliance causally reduces chronic addiction by promoting adherence to treatment and recovery, while decreasing therapy compliance directly increases the risk of continued chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '5cdbfe3d-f90f-4104-8143-cb756be353f5', 'judge_perplexity': 2.5349065474447273e+39, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10614854523557239, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that increased compliance with therapy routines leads to chronic addiction because individuals may become dependent on therapy itself, which allegedly reinforces addictive behaviors. Step by step:"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5756176423947427, 'motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance directly leads to chronic addiction because consistently following treatment routines can make individuals overly dependent on therapeutic interventions themselves, reinforcing addictive behaviors over time. As patients comply more with therapy, they may become habituated to the process, thus exacerbating their addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased therapy compliance directly leads to chronic addiction because consistently following treatment routines can make individuals overly dependent on therapeutic interventions themselves, reinforcing addictive behaviors over time. As patients comply more with therapy, they may become habituated to the process, thus exacerbating their addiction.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.647580546178818, 'j