103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3651191252423756, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause Time, nor does Time cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. They are conceptually independent, and changes in one do not causally produce changes in the other.', 'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.508176266631189, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08098577622140894, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation does not describe a plausible causal mechanism for how acute care demand leading to an ED visit could affect the passage of time; instead, it asserts the reverse (that time affects acute care demand and ED visits). The temporal ordering is entirely inappropriate, as it confuses cause and effect, suggesting reverse causation. The claim does not indicate that ED visits influence time in any substantial way, and the reasoning is circular and incoherent, conflating the direction of causality and failing all four criteria."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.668521797040931, 'motivation': "An increase in the passage of time directly causes a rise in acute care demand leading to more ED visits, because as time progresses, people's health naturally deteriorates, making emergency care inevitable. Therefore, simply waiting longer will always result in more ED visits due to the cumulative effects of aging and environmental exposures.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': "An increase in the passage of time directly causes a rise in acute care demand leading to more ED visits, because as time progresses, people's health naturally deteriorate