   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.4081770460434784, 'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause health literacy to change, nor does health literacy cause the availability of alternatives for ED to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables. Any association would be mediated by other factors such as education level or accessibility of information, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6315250353718562, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09330813298030247, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible causal mechanism\\u2014greater availability of alternatives increases exposure to health information, which could enhance health literacy (PLAUDIBILITY). It correctly asserts the temporal order (alternatives precede increased literacy) and avoids reverse causation (TEMPORALITY). However, it overstates the guarantee of improved literacy without addressing the strength or magnitude of the effect (STRENGTH), and the claim that exposure will \\"guarantee\\" better understanding is somewhat overconfident and weakens the internal logic (COHERENCE). Thus, while the pathway is plausible and temporally coherent, the explanation lacks nuance regarding effect size and internal consistency."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6710448795411947, 'motivation': 'Increased availability of alternatives for erectile dysfunction (ED) directly causes an improvement in health literacy, because as more options become accessible, individuals are naturally exposed to more health information and resources. This greater exposure guarantees that people will better understand ED and related health topics.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_