445. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.496723574594393, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-022-01227-z', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557971/', 'https://www.apa.org/monitor/2019/05/ce-corner-isolation'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08363335107683743, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible and specific causal mechanism (chronic stress hormone elevation weakening musculoskeletal systems) connecting feelings of fear and shame to frailty. Temporality is clearly established, with the causal direction running from emotional states to later physical frailty, and reverse causation is explicitly avoided. The strength of the relationship is emphasized by describing the pathway as \\"direct\\" and \\"primarily through biological stress pathways,\\" suggesting a substantial, not trivial, effect. The reasoning is coherent, internally consistent, and non-circular throughout the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.6395544082618064, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6865566094683508, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty through heightened physiological stress responses, such as chronic elevation of cortisol and other stress hormones, which directly weaken musculoskeletal systems over time. As individuals experience more fear and shame, these hormones continuously degrade muscle mass and bone density independent of behavioral factors, thereby accelerating the onset of frailty. Conversely, lower levels of fear and shame reduce chronic stress hormone exposure, directly preserving physical robustness and delaying frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased feelings of fear and shame cause increased frailty primarily through biological stress pathways.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty through heightened physiological stress responses, such as chronic elevation of cortisol and other stress hormones, which directly weaken musculoskeletal systems over time. As individuals experience more fear and shame, these hormones continuously degrade muscle mass and bone density independent of behavioral factors, thereby accelerating the onset of frailty. Conversely, lower levels of fear and shame reduce chronic stress hormone exposure, directly preserving physical robustness and delaying frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased feelings of fear and shame cause increased frailty primarily through biological stress pathways.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.05645507913402096, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7116577991056126, 'min_prob': 0.015865526587075164, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7594544835827695, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002476694227641096, 'prob_variance': 0.09940998216264513, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0013013251220690778, 'judge_prob_std': 0.28919431370073206, 'avg_nll': 0.5257935584806458, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1017876904918464, 'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty by promoting avoidance behaviors, social isolation, and reduced willingness to seek help, which in turn decrease physical and mental resilience. As feelings of fear and shame rise, individuals may neglect healthcare and healthy behaviors, accelerating frailty. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can support engagement in protective behaviors, reducing frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased fear and shame cause increased frailty.', 'prob_std': 0.3152934857599267, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'perplexit