   Properties: {'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5240185542503282, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0852644707427551, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation clearly describes a plausible neurobiological mechanism (stress hormones impairing cognition) linking fear and shame to medication errors (PLAUDIBILITY). The temporal direction is appropriate and unidirectional, with emotional states logically preceding incorrect medication use (TEMPORALITY). The explanation conveys that this effect is substantial by describing direct impairment of decision-making and memory, making the causal claim more than trivial (STRENGTH). Finally, the reasoning is internally consistent, non-circular, and logically coherent throughout (COHERENCE)."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.8061653757590002, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6875168193445668, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication through physiological stress responses that directly impair cognitive processing. When individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, stress hormones such as cortisol are released, which can disrupt memory and decision-making abilities. This neurobiological disruption increases the likelihood that individuals will forget proper dosage instructions or misunderstand medication guidelines, leading to incorrect use of medication. Reducing feelings of fear and shame would therefore enhance cognitive functioning and decrease the probability of medication errors.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication through physiological stress responses that directly impair cognitive processing. When individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, stress hormones such as cortisol are released, which can disrupt memory and decision-making abilities. This neurobiological disruption increases the likelihood that individuals will forget proper dosage instructions or misunderstand medication guidelines, leading to incorrect use of medication. Reducing feelings of fear and shame would therefore enhance cognitive functioning and decrease the probability of medication errors.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0240582735935686, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8718591821433379, 'min_prob': 0.06560587039319625, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7612546102975082, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023274757273088085, 'prob_variance': 0.04316762098379723, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.001071589135196998, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2920008060652489, 'avg_nll': 0.19341095477912404, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.258484619266178, 'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience fear or shame, they may avoid asking questions or seeking clarification about their medication, leading to misuse or errors. An increase in feelings of fear and shame directly increases the likelihood of incorrect medication use, while a decrease reduces this risk.', 'prob_std': 0.20776819050036804, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.2133813359318109, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8260841148748999}

447. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7121211521932433, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915321001347', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09656772600710604, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a specific (though somewhat simplistic) mechanism by referencing neural circuitry changes in emotional processing centers, meeting the plausibility criterion to some extent. Temporality is adequately addressed, as the causal direction is clear and unidirectional. However, the explanation overstates strength by implying inevitability (\\"inherently generate\\" and \\"automatically restore\\"), which is implausibly strong. It lacks coherence by disregarding environmental and social factors without justification and makes an internally inconsistent claim that risk is \\"eliminated\\" solely by reducing these feelings. Thus, it sufficiently addresses two criteria, but fails on strength and coherence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7308456627173514, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6170287629231201, 'motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the onset of a mental disorder by directly altering neural circuitry in the brain responsible for regulating psychological well-being. When individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, the brain's emotional processing centers, such as the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, undergo structural changes that inherently generate mental disorders, independent of any environmental or social factors. Thus, reducing feelings of fear and shame will automatically restore normal brain function and eliminate the risk of developing a mental disorder.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the onset of a mental disorder by directly altering neural circuitry in the brain responsible for regulating psychological well-being. When individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, the brain's emotional processing centers, such as the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, undergo structural changes that inherently generate mental disorders, independent of any environmental or social factors. Thus, reducing feelings of fear and shame will automatically restore normal brain function and eliminate the risk of developing a mental disorder.", 'judge_min_prob': 0.009799107561994102, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7868688322251902, 'min_prob': 0.037516945778342185, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.699927286408379, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026075752947268224, 'prob_variance': 0.07835833019655349, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0016657265660173894, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3107534810860629, 'avg_nll': 0.3701671456395873, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1994232025710696, 'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Increased feelings of fear and shame can elevate stress, social withdrawal, and negative self-perception, all of which are direct risk factors for mental health disorders. Conversely, reducing fear and shame may lower the risk or severity of mental disorders. This relationship is widely supported in psychological literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'prob_std': 0.2799255797467489, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.447976617415734, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0231882079924115}

