reby restricting the Availability of alternatives for ED.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased Financial resources among the population lead to a greater Availability of alternatives for ED because individuals with more financial means are less likely to require emergency department services, reducing overall demand and prompting healthcare systems to invest in alternative care options. Conversely, when Financial resources are limited, reliance on emergency departments rises, discouraging investment in alternative services and thereby restricting the Availability of alternatives for ED.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.011590465480621195, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8043718818012207, 'min_prob': 0.00793290701991003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7394590242324994, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002550786119833162, 'prob_variance': 0.07260394879215028, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0013281030337419701, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30751642275833624, 'avg_nll': 0.3771344733395992, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.0877057817192792, 'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause availability of alternatives for ED to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, more funding can be allocated to develop and maintain alternatives to emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care or community clinics. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources limits the options, reducing the availability of alternatives for ED.', 'prob_std': 0.2694511992776248, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4581003718148136, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8400960622514029}

513. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6708366637090888, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0936738907776498, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible psychological mechanism (perceived prestige and motivation) and maintains clear temporality (financial resources precede changes in functioning), so it meets those criteria. It also avoids reverse causation and is internally consistent without being circular. However, it does not convincingly establish the strength of the effect\\u2014while it describes immediate declines and improvements, it does not specify the magnitude or relative importance of financial resources compared to other factors, and may overstate the directness by ignoring material supports. Thus, it meets 2-3 criteria well but has notable gaps in conveying strength."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7718462361579738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6278261700452471, 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change through a psychological mechanism. As financial resources increase, healthcare professionals perceive their work environment as more prestigious and rewarding, which directly enhances their intrinsic motivation and cognitive performance, leading to improved functioning. Conversely, when financial resources decrease, professionals feel undervalued and demotivated, resulting in immediate declines in their ability to function effectively, regardless of actual changes in equipment or support.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change through a psychological mechanism. As financial resources increase, healthcare professionals perceive their work environment as more prestigious and rewarding, which directly enhances their intrinsic motivation and cognitive performance, leading to improved functioning. Conversely, when financial resources decrease, professionals feel undervalued and demotivated, resulting in immediate declines in their ability to function effectively, regardless of actual changes in equipment or support.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.042519678925720096, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8222556928687051, 'min_prob': 0.0996096587849355, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7123210693435268, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041286595564986525, 'prob_variance': 0.06823672432845078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0023893297334925344, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3060619067732046, 'avg_nll': 0.29374996843411894, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3139081429824748, 'original_motivation': "Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables access to better equipment, training, and support, which directly improves healthcare professionals' ability to function effectively. Conversely, decreasing financial resources restricts these supports and impairs functioning. This relationship is not mediated by another variable present in the CLD.", 'prob_std': 0.26122160004190076, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3414484572743044, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8644099741365858}

514. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 5.054271050253137e+39, 'citations': ['https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://www.acquisition.gov/far/2.101', 'https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-2/subtitle-A/chapter-II/part-200'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10169024817315321, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation fails the key criteria of PLAUSIBILITY and TEMPORALITY. It actually describes Equipment causing Financial resources (reverse causation), not Financial resources leading to more Equipment. The mechanism is circular and does not plausibly explain how Financial resources increases Equipment, but instead makes the opposite claim. While the explanation is fairly strong and internally consistent for the reverse direction, it does not address the claimed pathway or causal order, thus failing to meet the required criteria."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7838220295359924, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.63299432923344, 'motivation': 'Financial resources increase as a direct result of having more equipment, since the presence of extensive equipment signals organizational capability and efficiency to external stakeholders. This perceived capacity attracts greater investments and funding, thus enhancing financial resources. Conversely, a reduction in equipment diminishes stakeholder confidence, leading to decreased financial resources. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources increase as a direct result of having more equipment, since the presence of extensive equipment signals organizational capability and efficiency to external stakeholders. This perceived capacity attracts greater investments and funding, thus enhancing financial resources. Conversely, a reduction in equipment diminishes stakeholder confidence, leading to decreased financial resources. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8381380969731963, 'min_prob': 0.07844945694594645, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6984926807562876, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0040761388836613585, 'prob_variance': 0.05177211445815015, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.004004488187257361, 'judge_prob_std': 0.31888908443713343, 'avg_nll': 0.24732410119995005, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3077877024668552, 'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause equipment acquisition in a direct causal relationship. Increased financial resources enable the purchase or maintenance of more equipment, while decreased financial resources limit the ability to obtain or sustain equipment. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'prob_std': 0.22753486426952277, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.28059408760816, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0948450720594145}

515. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3594670149633064, 'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing financial resources directly reduces financial barriers, making it easier to access goods or services. Conversely, reducing financial resources increases financial barriers.', 'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7172655248249256, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09737609692981408, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation is plausible (it describes a mechanism: increased resources leading to stricter regulations that create new barriers) and maintains logical coherence (no circularity or contradiction). Temporality is also addressed, as the direction from increased resources to subsequent changes in barriers is clear. However, the explanation is weak on strength; it does not convey that this is a substantial or primary effect, nor does it assert more than a possible correlation. The language (\\"can actually lead,\\" \\"might introduce\\") is tentative, and it does not indicate the magnitude or importance of the effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6143783881242194, 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources can actually lead to an increase in financial barriers because organizations with more funds might introduce stricter regulations or more complex financial systems, inadvertently creating new obstacles. Therefore, greater financial resources could positively correlate with the existence of financial barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources can actually lead to an increase in financial barriers because organizations with more funds might introduce stricter regulations or more complex financial systems, inadvertently creating new obstacles. Therefore, greater financial resources could positively correlate with the existence of financial barriers.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.027120425453912966, 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6976117680445069, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0015686777807849795, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3120514331481496}

516. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1500587083837492, 'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause Personnel to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, organizations have more capacity to hire and retain personnel. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources leads to fewer available personnel due to budget constraints. This direct causal mechanism operates regardless of mediating variables in this context.', 'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6729241749040782, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10287360744837176, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible causal mechanism (automation/downsizing as a result of increased financial resources) and correctly supports the directionality (financial resources precede and lead to personnel changes), meeting the PLAUSIBILITY and TEMPORALITY criteria. However, the explanation does not clearly convey the strength or relative importance of this relationship\\u2014there is no indication that this effect is substantial or typical rather than occasional. The reasoning is internally consistent and coherent but lacks emphasis on the strength of the causal claim, preventing a fully correct assessment."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6693225753402094, 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources often leads to automation and downsizing, which reduces the need for personnel. Therefore, more financial resources can actually decrease the number of employees required, creating a negative relationship between financial resources and personnel.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources often leads to automation and downsizing, which reduces the need for personnel. Therefore, more financial resources can actually decrease the number of employees required, creating a negative relationship between financial resources and personnel.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.04047236369258869, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7185861353699817, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0029867923977095526, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3207391579591924}

517. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1986988574356428, 'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater financial resources enable access to better educational opportunities, materials, and environments, leading to higher education levels. Conversely, reduced financial resources limit educational access and attainment. This relationship is directional from financial resources to education level.', 'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6255158624525607, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09773863993525321, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible causal mechanism (reduced motivation and earlier workforce entry due to wealth), and maintains appropriate temporal direction (financial resources precede educational choices), meeting the PLAUSIBILITY and TEMPORALITY criteria. However, it is somewhat tentative about the STRENGTH of the effect, not clearly indicating that the influence is substantial rather than possible or context-dependent. The reasoning is COHERENT and non-circular, but overall, the explanation lacks emphasis on the magnitude or importance of the effect, resulting in a partially correct judgment."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6720686593006547, 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources can actually lower education levels because individuals with more money may feel less motivated to pursue higher education, relying instead on their wealth for success. Additionally, greater financial resources might encourage early entry into the workforce or family businesses, reducing the perceived need for further education.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT'