   Properties: {'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6955683503051404, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4131737/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10151559517583805, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a specific and temporally coherent pathway (healthcare professionals\\u2019 functioning influences patients\\u2019 neurocognitive development and, subsequently, health literacy), addressing PLAUSIBILITY and TEMPORALITY. However, the mechanism posited\\u2014direct neurocognitive development in adults via healthcare professional interaction\\u2014is implausible and lacks scientific grounding, undermining PLAUSIBILITY. The explanation does convey that the effect is important (STRENGTH), but the reasoning stretches plausibility and is not fully coherent, as it conflates knowledge transfer with intrinsic cognitive capacity, which may not be internally consistent (COHERENCE)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7529743721157138, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6521559506520881, 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change by directly influencing patients’ intrinsic cognitive abilities. When healthcare professionals function effectively, their expertise and authority stimulate neurocognitive development in patients, thereby enhancing the patients’ capacity to process and retain complex health information, which leads to increased health literacy. Conversely, reduced functioning of healthcare professionals can diminish this cognitive stimulation, resulting in lower health literacy among patients.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Functionin