oning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly because improved professional performance signals to patients that their health conditions are serious and warrant strict adherence to prescribed therapies. As healthcare professionals demonstrate high functioning through efficiency and technical skill, patients infer increased medical risk and thus become more compliant with therapy recommendations. Conversely, if functioning of healthcare professionals appears suboptimal, patients may perceive their conditions as less urgent, leading to decreased therapy compliance.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0169094261485113, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089498788297866, 'min_prob': 0.1444925402751354, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6929655971361296, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005881400000011003, 'prob_variance': 0.07242282580198672, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.002737774929374535, 'judge_prob_std': 0.31159381735608715, 'avg_nll': 0.29965421122100405, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2228229116634353, 'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'prob_std': 0.2691148933113638, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.349392122239041, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0558327096126396}

