613. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2040351903991457, 'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand and manage their health, which can prevent unnecessary acute care episodes and reduce ED visits. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of mismanaging health issues, leading to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.785941120101642, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11113478527169973, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible and temporally coherent mechanism (higher health literacy enables earlier recognition and help-seeking, so cause precedes effect). However, it is weak on strength\\u2014while it implies importance, it does not clearly argue that health literacy is a substantial influence relative to other factors or that the relationship is more than trivial. The reasoning is mostly coherent, but the claim that higher health literacy increases ED visits (rather than perhaps shifting demand to appropriate care settings) could introduce internal inconsistency or oversimplification."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6421670277102759, 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to better recognize the seriousness of their symptoms and empower them to seek medical attention quickly, resulting in more appropriate and timely use of emergency departments. Therefore, as health literacy increases, demand for acute care and ED visits would also increase because people are more likely to seek help at the first sign of illness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to better recognize the seriousness of their symptoms and empower them to seek medical attention quickly, resulting in more appropriate and timely use of emergency departments. Therefore, as health literacy increases, demand for acute care and ED visits would also increase because people are more likely to seek help at the first sign of illness.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.028581625139603366, 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7056828588765083, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.003432837654778258, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3333688426828454}

