c addiction. Conversely, the absence of healthy lifestyle habits fails to induce these protective physiological changes, increasing susceptibility to chronic addiction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease because individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle develop physiological adaptations that make their bodies less receptive to the neurochemical effects of addictive substances. For instance, regular exercise and balanced nutrition are thought to alter neurotransmitter pathways in a way that directly blocks the rewarding sensations of addictive behaviors. As a result, those practicing a healthy lifestyle experience diminished pleasure from substances, leading to a natural decline in chronic addiction. Conversely, the absence of healthy lifestyle habits fails to induce these protective physiological changes, increasing susceptibility to chronic addiction.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.018672554764173167, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8048516824590491, 'min_prob': 0.04028507003715813, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7045010931834289, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032869299953729924, 'prob_variance': 0.060895131692964406, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0026978429640193355, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3179691778945151, 'avg_nll': 0.30413312692495276, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3167050087642327, 'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in behaviors such as regular exercise, balanced nutrition, and avoiding harmful substances reduces the risk and persistence of chronic addiction. Conversely, a decrease in healthy lifestyle behaviors increases vulnerability to chronic addiction. This relationship is inverse and direct, not merely correlational.', 'prob_std': 0.24676938970010928, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.35544