   Properties: {'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6740907892732548, 'citations': ['https://www.acep.org/siteassets/sites/acep/media/crowding/empc_crowding-ip_092016.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0973179456316763, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible mechanism (exposure to diverse health information and behaviors affects perception of urgency), and maintains logical coherence without circularity. Temporality is reasonably justified, as social network size is presented as preceding health-seeking behavior. However, the explanation does not convincingly establish the strength or importance of the causal relationship, using tentative language and not clarifying whether this is a substantial influence rather than a minor or correlational one."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7696067283392602, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6137953947357445, 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Individuals with a larger social network are exposed to a greater diversity of health information and health behaviors, which increases their awareness of non-urgent symptoms and enables them to self-diagnose more accurately. As a result, they are less likely to perceive the need for acute care and are therefore less likely to seek care at an ED, regardless of their actual health status. Conversely, those with a smaller social network receive less health information and remain unaware of minor symptoms, leading them to interpret any health concern as urgent and increasing their likelihood