n': 'Willingness of social network is directly influenced by the accessibility of information, as individuals within a social network become more willing to engage and participate when information is readily accessible. Enhanced accessibility of information fosters trust and openness, which subsequently increases the willingness of the social network to interact and share. Conversely, when accessibility of information is limited, members are less motivated to exhibit willingness, resulting in decreased engagement within the network.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.007556682199357082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8500595812809894, 'min_prob': 0.038030067380463356, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7234707933032506, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00476626663360866, 'prob_variance': 0.06059869929573389, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.004462728711548303, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32537414764307704, 'avg_nll': 0.2587639069230133, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3193610961732238, 'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes accessibility of information to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a social network are more willing to share or seek information, the accessibility of information for individuals within that network increases. Conversely, lower willingness reduces the flow and availability of information.', 'prob_std': 0.24616803061269732, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.295327950762853, 'max_window_entropy': 0.9179614848169106}

