1097. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5561270385004822, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3194102/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22016701/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08284218795320633, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation articulates a plausible causal mechanism (PLAUDIBILITY) by specifying that increased willingness in the social network heightens social scrutiny and self-monitoring, plausibly deterring addiction. TEMPORALITY is addressed: the direction is clear (social network willingness precedes changes in addiction) and reverse causation is not implied. However, the STRENGTH of the relationship is only implied as \\"direct\\" but not clearly argued as substantial or important, and it dismisses all other factors without justification. COHERENCE is mostly present, but the claim that the relationship is \\"direct\\" and \\"not contingent on individual psychological factors\\" slightly undermines internal consistency, as self-monitoring is itself a psychological process."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7333978978611814, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6727674736926975, 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. As the willingness of a social network increases, individuals may become more aware of the negative perceptions and expectations of their peers, which heightens their self-monitoring and deters them from engaging in addictive behaviors, thereby directly reducing chronic addiction. Conversely, decreased willingness of a social network removes this social scrutiny, making individuals less likely to regulate their substance use and thus increasing the severity or persistence of chronic addiction. This relationship is direct and is not contingent on individual psychological factors or external interventions.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. As the willingness of a social network increases, individuals may become more aware of the negative perceptions and expectations of their peers, which heightens their self-monitoring and deters them from engaging in addictive behaviors, thereby directly reducing chronic addiction. Conversely, decreased willingness of a social network removes this social scrutiny, making individuals less likely to regulate their substance use and thus increasing the severity or persistence of chronic addiction. This relationship is direct and is not contingent on individual psychological factors or external interventions.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.01886705298987902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7909097783809855, 'min_prob': 0.03272460153875574, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7522920276412036, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004543330407857183, 'prob_variance': 0.0733226190674597, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0010903153794808325, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2878231886996014, 'avg_nll': 0.35421849798381283, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.181808372366641, 'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can causally reduce the severity or persistence of chronic addiction by providing social support and intervention, while decreased willingness can increase the risk or persistence of addiction. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'prob_std': 0.27078149690748754, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation'