   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.143590426803962, 'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes informal care in a direct causal relationship. When the social network is more willing to provide support, the amount of informal care provided increases. Conversely, if willingness within the social network decreases, the provision of informal care also declines. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '39d4c582-4cdc-4641-859a-27818d9c9cba', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6737071365787233, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0932497168173629, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible and coherent causal mechanism (\\"diffusion of responsibility\\" leading to reduced informal care), and the temporal direction is correctly specified (willingness precedes and supposedly affects care behavior). However, the explanation does not clearly convey the strength or importance of this effect; it does not specify whether this mechanism is substantial or merely a minor influence. Overall, the account is internally consistent and non-circular, but lacks emphasis on the magnitude or significance of the causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6540024636375453, 'motivation': 'A higher willingness of a social network can actually reduce the provision of informal care because individuals may assume that someone else will take responsibility, leading to a diffusion of responsibility. As a result, the actual amount of informal care provided may decrease when more people express willingness, due to lowered personal accountability.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher willingness of a social network can actually reduce the provision of informal care because individuals may assume that someone else will take responsibility, leading to a diffusion of responsibility. As a result, the actual amount of informal care provided may decrease when more people express willingness, due to lowered personal accountability.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.003577503675194805, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7326115630171347, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.002358402571379227, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30536816601827194}

