ain physical and cognitive health. Conversely, a reduction in willingness of social network may compel individuals to become more self-reliant, thereby actively reducing their frailty through increased independence and self-care. This relationship is direct, as the availability of support is assumed to modulate activity levels that influence frailty outcomes.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of social network to assist may directly decrease frailty by fostering a sense of dependency, which in turn leads individuals to engage less frequently in activities that maintain physical and cognitive health. Conversely, a reduction in willingness of social network may compel individuals to become more self-reliant, thereby actively reducing their frailty through increased independence and self-care. This relationship is direct, as the availability of support is assumed to modulate activity levels that influence frailty outcomes.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0062437473747812815, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8061095171898024, 'min_prob': 0.06607390366042182, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.646245810229682, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034676632007529886, 'prob_variance': 0.06760867200618924, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0027251294164692213, 'judge_prob_std': 0.34074744462254997, 'avg_nll': 0.3186179831860921, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3744358467387912, 'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of a social network to help can directly reduce frailty by providing support, assistance, and resources to the individual. Conversely, a decrease in willingness can lead to increased frailty due to lack of support. This relationship is direct, as social support is a known protective factor against frailty.', 